{
  "title": "Adding Error Bars to Evals: A Statistical Approach to Language Model Evaluations",
  "authors": [
    "Evan Miller"
  ],
  "year": 2024,
  "arxivId": "2411.00640",
  "abstract": "Evaluations of language models are often presented as simple scores, but this can be misleading. By treating evaluations as experiments and applying statistical techniques, we can add error bars to these scores and better understand the uncertainty and variability in language model performance. This paper presents a framework for analyzing evaluation data, comparing models, and determining the required sample size for detecting significant differences.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "When evaluating language models, we're essentially conducting an experiment. By recognizing this, we can apply statistical techniques to better understand the results. <strong>Evaluations are not just about achieving the highest score, but about understanding the uncertainty and variability in performance</strong>. This paper introduces a framework for adding error bars to evaluation scores, enabling more informed comparisons between models.",
